[{:seon.task/name "an-agents-effect-budget-survives-a-restart"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (was Optimization). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: seon.fx/owner-proc (src/seon/fx.clj:1591) starts its state with :spent {} (fx.clj:1608); admit (fx.clj:1483) refuses :seon.fx/budget-spent when spent? (fx.clj:1477) finds (get-in st [:spent budget-key]) at or past the request's :budget; charge (fx.clj:1497) adds an outcome's :cost. Nothing restores :spent, so a root restart forgets every key's spend. my.fx/call takes {:budget n :budget-key k} (src/my/fx.clj:7); no production caller sets a budget [M, git grep]. Each model call is already a :seon.model-call entity with :seon.model-call/tokens (resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn:753). The cause: a decision read from a counter only one process remembers, where the store holds the facts it sums. Design, on the one effects owner (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer: admission a pure fn of (config, state) in owner-proc): a budgeted request carries the spend its asker read from the store at its ask (the agent sums its own model calls on its branch with one pull, computed where read, never stored); the owner adds only what it charged since, held per in-flight :seon.step/request-id and dropped at the answer, so its state holds in-flight charges only and a restart loses nothing a store read does not give back [H: the sum's home is the agent's branch, settled when the pull is tried at a REPL]. A non-model effect that charges cost writes its cost as its own fact first, or is not budgeted. Deletes: owner-proc's :spent map and its :ping-map-fn entry (fx.clj:1607); spent? folds into admit. Estimate: n = the key's calls, at most ~1,000; one pull per budgeted request, ~1M ticks at n = 1,000, O(n); none for a request with no budget; admit stays O(1). No law guards this class alone: a proc's state being in-flight or derived is the Flow standard's rule (seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each), checked there. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/an-agents-budget-holds-across-a-restart (spend a budget, start a fresh owner-proc state, the next call over it is refused :seon.fx/budget-spent; red today: admitted)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/an-agents-budget-holds-across-a-restart}}
 {:seon.task/name "deps-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the :deps effect (`seon.fx/resolve-deps`) resolves a deps map through Jolt's ``jolt.deps/resolve-deps`` and answers the source roots or :seon.fx/unresolved; there is no add-lib or sync-deps at the REPL and no checksum (docs/source.md#dependencies; leaves them out). Correct, with Clojure's names (clojure.repl.deps): add-lib, add-libs and sync-deps ask the :deps effect through `my.fx/call`, load the answered roots in the agent's process and record the coordinate on its branch; a resolved jar or git tree is checked against a sha256 recorded at its first resolve, and a mismatch is the effect's typed error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/add-lib-loads-a-resolved-library seon.fx-test/a-changed-artifact-is-refused-by-its-checksum}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-effect-opens-a-receipt"
  :seon.task/plan "Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: an agent holds an effect in flight only as an entry of its process-global state, (swap! state update :fx-pending assoc k kind) in seon.agent/fx! (src/seon/agent.clj:493-512), keyed by :k from :tx-seq; no effect fact is written (no :seon.fx/status in resources/seon/schemas), so an agent that dies with a :file, :git or :model call in flight leaves no record that it asked, and a resumed branch cannot tell sent from unsent. v1 committed a receipt before the handler ran, settled it once, and never re-sent it on recovery (~/src/seon/src/seon/effect.clj). Design, on the one effects owner (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer): the receipt is the request: for a kind whose schema entry says it is not idempotent, the agent writes the effect entity on its branch (:seon.step/request-id, the one request id that replaces fx's :dest {:agent :op :k} and the agent's :tx-seq :k; kind; request; the asking eval's ordinal; :seon.fx/status :open) before fx! sends; the answer settles it and links the eval; the reconciler marks a dead agent's open receipts :interrupted and never re-sends them. The :fx-pending entry goes (H2 of runtime's Flow survey: the transport's keys leave the global atom). Deletes: :fx-pending and :tx-seq in seon.agent/state for effects. Estimate: one datom set per non-idempotent effect (~6 datoms, ~50k ticks, within a write the eval makes anyway); none for an idempotent read. Data owns the schema entry; runtime builds the owner side. Foundation for seon.fx/files-and-shell-for-any-work. Proofs, red first: the two below (no receipt today, so both are red)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-dead-agents-open-effect-is-interrupted-not-resent seon.fx-test/an-effect-links-its-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "files-and-shell-for-any-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Seon does any work its user wants (AGENTS §1; owner 2026-10-22). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: resources/seon/system.edn's :fx (system.edn:64-89) has twelve kinds (echo file source git deps build tests item history evidence embed model), each with a :workers count; seon.fx/write-file writes any path with no prior-digest check, and seon.repl's :path place calls it directly, not through the owner (src/seon/repl.clj:976-992); there is no my.fs, my.edit or my.shell (src/my/). Processes start four ways [M]: seon.lib/sh (src/seon/lib.clj:11, synchronous, on a fiber), seon.vector/start!'s raw ProcessBuilder (src/seon/vector.clj:427), run-item's zsh -c strings around bin scripts (src/seon/fx.clj:1427-1447), and bin/held (an edge). Design, in line with the rulings (one process-start primitive owned by the effects owner's :process kind; every effect wait bounded by its declared :seon.bound/bound): :shell is that primitive's synchronous case (argv only, cwd, an env allowlist, a stdin cap, its own process group, niced, bounded by bytes and by :seon.bound/ms, killed whole past it, its answer :seon.fx/indeterminate when killed); :fs (read, glob, stat, write with the prior digest as precondition) and :edit (exact-span text and line windows) are schema entries on the same owner; my.fs, my.edit and my.shell are thin wrappers over my.fx/call. v1's handler policy becomes spec'd config keys (:seon.config.fs/*, :seon.config.shell/env-allowlist; v1 src/seon/fs/jvm.clj, shell/jvm.clj); each function gets a section in docs/agent.md. Deletes: write-file's unchecked path, folded into the :fs write; repl's :path place calls the :fs kind. Estimate: an :fs read is one file read plus a round trip through the owner (~50k ticks [H], measured against a direct call by the round-trip cost proof of seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer); a shell adds one spawn (20-35 ms from Jolt, docs/machine.md#processes). Proofs, red first: the three below (none of the kinds exists today)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-write-needs-the-prior-digest seon.fx-test/shell-answers-exit-and-digested-output seon.fx-test/shell-sees-only-allowed-env}}
 {:seon.task/name "web-fetch-and-search"
  :seon.task/plan "Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: no :web kind in resources/seon/system.edn's :fx (system.edn:64-89), so an agent cannot fetch a page or search (parity.md gap 11); the model key is read only by root's :model owner (seon.fx/model, src/seon/fx.clj, the provider names its env variable, never the key). v1: my.web/fetch and search, http(s) only, no userinfo in the URL, manual redirects with loop detection, a body cap (~/src/seon/src/seon/web/jvm.clj); the search key held by root, named by an env-var config key. Design, on the one effects owner (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer): :seon.fx.web/fetch and :seon.fx.web/search are two schema entries, each with its :seon.bound/bound declared (:ms, measured then x10; :bytes the body cap; :items the redirect count, a loop or a hop past it answered :seon.bound/reached), outside reads recorded with their digest; the search key read only by the owner's perform, as the model's is; my.web a wrapper over my.fx/call; caps as :seon.config.web/* keys. Deletes nothing; adds no proc (owner-proc per kind). Estimate: one HTTP round trip plus the owner's round trip; bytes bounded by the cap. Proofs, red first: the two below (no kind today)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/redirects-are-bounded seon.fx-test/search-key-stays-in-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tree-run-is-one-function"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: three runners of a command in a tree, not two: seon.fx/build (src/seon/fx.clj:760: bin/worktree --at under generations-dir, then nice bin/build seon through zsh -c with a log), seon.fx/run-on-checkout (fx.clj:1156-1320, 165 lines: a detached worktree under tests-dir, the candidate's files written over it, root's binary and stores cloned in, prove-tree or a built run, the worktree removed), and seon.fx/run-item (fx.clj:1387: :build, :seed and :step items on a session's checkout through zsh -c strings around bin/build, bin/held, bin/system seed, bin/check --steps). The rulings change the answer: proving is the effects owner's :process kind and a merge's check is a prove of the candidate branch in the system, with no worktree and no gate rebuild (docs/plan.md Stage 1; seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer (6); seon.reconcile/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch). So run-on-checkout and run-item's :seed and :step are deleted by those tasks, not merged; what is left is one in-tree run, a build of a tree's runtime when its pins or native code differ, which seon.fx/runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch asks. Design: seon.fx/build stays the one function that runs a command in a tree, through the :process kind's start (its group, its bound, niced); run-item's :build case calls it. Deletes: run-on-checkout (165 lines) and run-item's :seed and :step cases, with the :process kind's landing; the generations-dir against tests-dir split. Estimate: a deletion, src about -250 lines; a build's cost unchanged (bin/build seon, 25-45 s, system.edn:69). Design guard, red first: seon.main-test/one-form-runs-a-command-in-a-tree, from the compiler's facts: the src forms whose source names bin/build or bin/worktree are exactly seon.fx/build (red today: run-on-checkout and run-item)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.main-test/one-form-runs-a-command-in-a-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "tree-id-and-inputs-spawn-only-what-they-need"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by the slow-test helpers. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: seon.fx/inputs (src/seon/fx.clj:478) now takes the tree its caller holds (its 4-arity; bin/test's plan computes it once) and reads every pin with one git ls-tree -r of it (fx.clj:496), so inputs' 23 children are down to the ls-tree plus a status of each dirty pin [H: the count to measure]; seon.fx/tree-id (fx.clj:382) still spawns several git children a call (rev-parse of the index path, the index copy, add -A, write-tree) and test-runner/program-db calls tree-id when not given one (test/test_runner.clj:201), and the plan's analysis calls it once more (test_runner.clj:151). A child spawned from Jolt costs 20-35 ms (docs/machine.md#processes). Superseded in direction by the one derivation: a branch's pins are datoms (seon.source/an-import-writes-each-gitlink-as-an-entity) and seon.fx/runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch reads them from the db, so inputs' pin walk goes with that; this task keeps the slice that is left until then: tree-id takes HEAD from its caller when held, and no test reading the program spawns a git child. Estimate: a program-reading test's children ~7 to 0; an inputs call to 1 plus one a dirty pin, constant in the number of pins. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/inputs-spawns-children-constant-in-the-pins (seon.lib/sh counted over an inputs call on a checkout with 2 and with 6 pins: the same count). No law fits the class (a cost, held by the proof's budget)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/inputs-spawns-children-constant-in-the-pins}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-verdict-caches-size-ledger-counts-a-rewrite-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by a slow-test helper (group B): seon.fx/evict!'s size ledger adds a rewritten verdict path's whole size again, so the ledger drifts upward past the cache's real size and evicts early. Correct: a write's ledger delta is its new size less the old file's. Proof, red first: rewriting one verdict ten times leaves the ledger at its size once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
