 {:seon.task/name "a-systems-items-end-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-12 (Optimization, the itemseed trial): bin/system stop ended a worktree system proving a tree through its items while two process items and a check-step item ran; they kept running, and their shards kept starting test roots, until 12 process groups were ended by hand. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. What exists [M]: seon.fx/run-item (src/seon/fx.clj:1387) runs bin/build, zsh -c (bin/held, bin/system seed) and bin/check --steps through seon.lib/sh in root's own process group, with no bound (bin/bounded is no longer called from src: git grep); seon.system/stop! (src/seon/system.clj:2388) drains the procs and kills the fork server, and ends nothing an item started; a process started from the binary ends with its parent (seon.lib/end-with!, src/seon/main.clj:169), but bin/check and bin/test are shell scripts and do not watch theirs. The cause: an effect's process is not held by its owner. Design, a slice of the :process kind (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer (3)): the one process-start primitive puts each process in its own group (setpgid) and the owner keeps {request-id {:pgid}} in its state; the owner's :stop transition kills every running group (seon.lib/kill! of the negative pgid) and answers each request :seon.system/ended before the system answers its stop, the same path as a bound reached. Deletes: bin/bounded, and bin/end-tree's use for items. Estimate: n running items at most the core count; one kill(2) per group at stop, O(n), microseconds each. Proofs, red first: seon.fx-test/a-systems-items-end-with-it (a system with a long process item is stopped; no process of that item's group outlives the stop); the design guard seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds (no src form outside seon.lib's start path names java.lang.ProcessBuilder or clojure.java.shell/sh, so a process its owner does not group cannot be started; red today: seon.vector/start!, vector.clj:427)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-systems-items-end-with-it seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner review accepted: the bound is declared on the effect's request schema, a call or scope can only shorten it, Flow enforces ticks and bytes per message, the effects owner enforces wall time and child memory; no :seon.fx/workers (the schema says :seon.fx/needs #{:cpu :memory :io} and :seon.fx/exclusive; the one admission decides from cores and memory); shell defaults to :seon.fx/reuse :never unless its request declares its inputs, then :by-reads; effects are routed at Jolt's host primitives (process spawn, file open, socket connect, env read, clock read) inside agent processes, none in root's bootstrap; every default bound states its reason from measured runs; outside reads route automatically; defaults are a malli :default property, overrides documented config keys through the one resolver. The owner, 2026-11-14: \"No gates or fences unless it's at boot or an edge ... I want the system to operate via flow\"; and the machine's share: work stays niced and admitted to the core count, less while he uses the machine heavily, and once proving is Flow a runtime value the system responds to live.\n\nWhat exists, read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: the owner is seon.fx/owner-proc (src/seon/fx.clj:1591), one per kind in system.edn's :fx (resources/seon/system.edn:64-89, twelve kinds, each with :workers), its workers made by count (seon.fx/procs, fx.clj:1812), admit by :busy against :workers (fx.clj:1493). A second admission is seon.prove's (admission prove.clj:82, config-admission :96, grant :240) with held-processes (prove.clj:118, one ps read of every process's environment each grant pass); a third is bin/test's shard count. The bound's first piece landed: :seon.config.fx/bounds (resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.ai.edn:72; resources/seon/config/default.edn:797 bounds only :model, 240 s, ten times the slowest of 120 measured calls); seon.agent/fx! waits a non-streamed request's :seon.bound/ms (src/seon/agent.clj:493-512); a stream's bound is of quiet, re-armed per line (seon.fx/stream-due, fx.clj:1711), proved by seon.fx-test/a-stream-that-goes-quiet-past-its-kinds-bound-is-ended-reached. Processes start four ways: seon.lib/sh (src/seon/lib.clj:11), seon.vector/start!'s raw ProcessBuilder (src/seon/vector.clj:427), run-item's zsh -c strings (fx.clj:1427-1447), bin/held; ``seon.fx/stall-evidence`` runs an inline zsh script (fx.clj:1832) beside bin/evidence. Test children fork through jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! (agent.clj:1353), not seon.lib/fork!, with an io-thread per child for its exit (agent.clj:1357). Seven deadline mechanisms (root's :stall-check timer system.clj:939 and the agent proc's :stall? agent_proc.clj:122; bin/stalls; bin/bounded; bin/check's watchdog; seon.agent/arm-wall!; seon.proofs' step-end bound; bin/held --end). Three request-id schemes (step's :rid/:tag, fx's :dest {:agent :op :k}, the agent's :k from :tx-seq).\n\nDesign (runtime builds the owner, its ports, admission, the deadline, the group kill and the process primitive; tests builds the run's request, the child's per-test result, the answer's verdicts and the merge check; runtime reviews): (1) One effects owner: owner-proc per kind; no new proc. Proving is a :process kind on it; its request :seon.fx.process/request {:seon.step/request-id, :seon.process/env {:branch :commit :t}, :seon.fx/run (test names or a check step), :seon.bound/bound}, answered exactly once by :seon.fx/answer (verdicts plus :seon.bound/spent, or a typed error). (2) Admission is a pure fn of (config, state) in owner-proc, replacing :workers for every kind: the share :seon.config.proofs/max-cores of :seon.config.machine/cores, free memory, and each running class's weight, its measured peak processes (:seon.fx/peak-processes, seon.lib/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once); read through the one resolver at each admission, so a lowered share applies at the next one and runs in flight finish by their own bounds; a full in-port is the back-pressure. (3) Every effect wait is bounded: the effective bound is the least per key of the schema's :default, the request's :seon.bound/bound and the config override; the owner enforces :ms with one seon.step/put-after! per request, kills the group and answers :seon.bound/reached, never a cached red; every kind gets a measured bound in :seon.config.fx/bounds. (4) One process-start primitive owned by the :process kind: spawn in its own group, niced, its bound, its end as :seon.process/end; seon.lib/sh is its synchronous case; vector's ProcessBuilder and run-item's zsh -c route to it; stall-evidence calls bin/evidence through it; bin/held stays only as the edge for a root that must outlive its starter, until the online takeover. (5) Test children fork through seon.lib/fork! (one exit discipline and one fork-wait bound), the link made in :child; the exit watch becomes the agent's tests proc's in-port fed by jolt.io-poller/exit-chan, no thread per child. A dead child is an error as data on its answer (:seon.process/end with its signal), never a rerun: run-children!'s respawn goes. (6) Verdicts are cached only from a process whose hold-env! held the request's env; a pin or native difference answers :seon.process/env-mismatch, which asks the build (seon.fx/runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch). (7) A merge's check is the same request on the candidate branch's env: no worktree, no gate rebuild. (8) One request id, :seon.step/request-id, replacing fx's :dest and the agent's :tx-seq :k. (9) No timer outside Flow: put-after! only.\n\nDeletes: :workers (system.edn and seon.fx/procs); the :item and :tests kinds; seon.prove's grant, admission and held-processes (prove.clj:82-260); bin/bounded, bin/stalls and bin/check's watchdog (tests deletes the bin uses); root's :stall-check timer and :stall?; stall-evidence's inline script; arm-wall! once (5) lands; run-on-checkout and the merge gate's worktree and rebuild; run-children!'s respawn; the echo kind in production.\n\nEstimate: an effect's round trip through its owner is two channel puts plus the perform, ~50k ticks beyond the perform [H], held by a cost proof against a direct call; an admission is O(running) per decision, running at most the core count; one put-after! per request (~1k ticks) replaces a stall poll every stall-ms/2 per busy agent and the ps read (~70 ms a process) each grant pass.\n\nProofs, red first: the nine the owner accepted, below, and three design guards, each a query of the compiler's facts like seon.main-test/every-shell-runs-through-lib-sh: seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds (no src form outside seon.lib's start path names java.lang.ProcessBuilder or clojure.java.shell/sh; red today: seon.vector/start!); seon.main-test/every-fork-is-lib-fork (no src form but seon.lib/fork! names jolt.host/fork-alone or jolt.async.remote.flow/fork!; red today: seon.agent's fork-test-child); seon.main-test/every-effect-kind-declares-its-bound (every kind system.edn's :fx names has a :seon.config.fx/bounds entry; red today: eleven of twelve have none)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-call-can-only-shorten-its-bound seon.fx-test/a-child-memory-is-measured-at-exit seon.fx-test/a-lowered-core-share-admits-less-at-the-next-admission seon.fx-test/a-model-answer-is-recorded-never-reused seon.fx-test/a-reload-with-no-effect-runs-and-one-with-an-effect-is-refused-unless-accepted seon.fx-test/a-shell-past-its-ms-is-killed-whole-and-may-have-happened seon.fx-test/a-step-is-bounded-by-its-messages-bound seon.fx-test/an-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry seon.fx-test/an-outside-read-is-recorded-and-reused-while-its-digest-holds seon.main-test/every-effect-kind-declares-its-bound seon.main-test/every-fork-is-lib-fork seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"}}
 {:seon.task/name "runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch", :seon.task/plan "DESIGN, approved by root 2026-11-14 (estimate first, owner's rule): two identities. (1) BINARY: a digest of exactly what only a build can change, the build's own inputs. They are stated once, as the data bin/build runs from (resources/seon/config/build.edn: each step and the paths and pins it reads), so nothing restates them: seon.system/build-input? (a hand list: deps.edn, bin/build, .gitmodules, native/, resources/seon/config/, three env classes) is derived from that file and deleted. One implementation computes the digest (git blob ids of those paths plus the pin entities), called by bin/build (written beside the binary as target/seon.digest, replacing target/seon.tree as an identity) and by seon.fx/runtime-for at the one start. A mismatch builds once. (2) PROGRAM: the branch's forms as datoms; at the one start runtime's load-diff compares the program the binary recorded (target/seon.program) with the branch's and loads exactly the differing forms, O(d). Estimate: (1) one digest of ~20 paths and 16 pins at start, ~ms, no build unless a build input moved; (2) d form loads (2.46M ticks each today; eval-by-digest in the Jolt pin cuts that). Rejected: forms in the binary digest (every src edit would rebuild target/seon, ~93 s + ~40 s warm, measured 2026-11-14). CORE (root, from the owner: \"this shouldn't be possible\", a run on a binary its branch no longer matches; moved up to right after the fork bound, ahead of the Datahike pin): a binary's identity is the digest of exactly what it is built from (the branch's forms, its pins, deps.edn). It is current iff that digest equals its branch's, checked once at the one start; a mismatch builds. Evidence [M]: three checks disagreed on data's land: the plan said \"current by reach\" and built nothing, then bin/check refused the same binary. A second instance [M, data from the store]: on agent lb76's branch :main/task-seon-repl-an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval, tx 536878038 (2026-11-13T14:05:44Z), an eval under runtime fingerprint dev-DE9D22-9D4CA8D1-2114B5.CCCC55EA.111D38.744A1C9B-tr retracted only :var/form of seon.system/baked-paths, which main's import had deleted whole three minutes earlier (tx 536877923, a fingerprint without -tr): the branch recorded main's deletion as its own unmap, and its dead form turned seon.main-test/every-name-a-form-uses-is-a-var red there (reproduce: seon.system/branch-db and a history read of var eid 241742351592924184). The binary has two identities today: target/seon.tree (bin/build's record; bin/check looked for e4763c1e-1ebb3f39.all.edn) and target/seon.program (seon.program/binary-program, which the plan keys its judgements by); they become one digest, and both files' readers ask it. Deletes (owner: find fixes and delete the other implementations): \"current by reach\" (seon.system/stale-binary, seon.system/binary-current's reach), seon.problems/stale-binary's own check, the second identity file, bin/build current, test-runner/base-products and bin/land's own rebuild guess. Evidence of the class [M]: 2026-11-14, bin/land merged a moved main (34ca38f4a9) into data's tip without rebuilding target/seon, so seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed read a stale binary and went red, a false red that cost a land cycle; the same day a pin change took 3 tries to prove (bin/prove refused it on the standing system, then found its runtime unbuilt). So after any merge, bin/land builds what the merged tree names, once, through runtime-for. Agreed with runtime and the owner, 2026-11-14 (plans/deps-runtime-for.md). What exists: three guesses at the runtime a tree needs (bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, bin/land's bin/build jolt) and the ls-tree read of gitlinks (src/seon/fx.clj:175-214). Change: seon.fx/runtime-for [env] reads the pins from the branch's db (the gitlink entities), compares target/<step>.stamp with them, and answers the built runtime, or builds it, or :seon.process/env-mismatch naming the fields (data's error schema); runtime's start-process! calls it as the one check. Deletes: bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, the ls-tree read. Done: every runner asks runtime-for; a law goes red on one that execs target/seon or target/jolt without it. docs/merging.md#a-branch-lands-with-binland.\nTHE REFUSAL IS A GUARD (the owner via root, 2026-11-16; filed by flow for deps and runtime): seon.fx/runtime-for's :seon.process/env-mismatch refusing an agent's start is a guard to delete, not to move. With agents forked by the system process at main's program, each loading its branch's diff (seon.main/the-binary-main-is-the-system-process; seon.prove/tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent), a branch whose pins, binary or config differ from main's is an unmerged entry its own agent resolves (my.branch/resolve!), never a refused start. EVIDENCE [M, 2026-11-16]: session/flow's attach refused three times after main moved pins (differs: reference-code/lmdb, konserve, jolt, datahike, persistent-sorted-set, :seon.config.root/gc-generations), each repaired only by hand at root's REPL (docs/merging.md#root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl's settle form), a REPL the writer process is losing (seon.step/roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph, B2).", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-process-on-another-runtime-answers-env-mismatch seon.main-test/every-exec-of-a-built-runtime-asks-runtime-for}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scripts-environment-is-stated-in-one-place", :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-14 (deps): a shell that had sourced bin/env ran bin/jolt-gates, its JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS {:image-fn-forms false} reached seven Jolt gates and turned them red, and its HOME broke a git commit. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: bin/env still assigns as ${X:-default}, so a caller's value wins (bin/env:8, 9, 13, 16, 21); resources/seon/environment.edn does not exist; seon.fx's child-env-scrub and root-variables (src/seon/fx.clj:137-152) are a second hand list of the same variables (runtime's hand-made-lists audit: a DIAL, spec'd config). Agreed with runtime: one resources/seon/environment.edn, as data, each variable's name, :role (:seon or :jolt), :derived or :caller with the reason, and its schema (data's); config and pins come from the branch. bin/env clears JOLT_ and SEON_ and sets each variable from that file; runtime's once-at-start check (:seon.process/env-mismatch, the :process kind's start) and the child env scrub read the same file. No new script, runner or law (owner, 2026-11-14: \"No more heavy machinery on the claude side\"). Deletes: child-env-scrub's and root-variables' literal lists, derived from the file. Estimate: one EDN read at a start (~1M ticks), cached by the file's digest. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/a-script-takes-only-the-environment-it-is-given. Plan: plans/deps-script-environment.md. docs/machine.md#processes.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-script-takes-only-the-environment-it-is-given}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-file-or-git-read-goes-through-the-one-catch-up"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by deps for runtime (its one catch-up), root's call, 2026-11-14. Root's one-tracker direction, agreed 2026-11-14 (target/root/one-derivation-survey.html): every read a computation makes records through Datahike's di/record-read! as a datom key, so a reaction or a reused verdict is never green on a read nobody recorded (a false green). What exists [M, counts in src]: 31 (slurp ...) calls and 49 git!/lib/sh calls read the outside world directly, past any recorder. Change: the outside is read only by the one catch-up, which turns it into datoms (:file/text, :seon.pin, git facts); every other site reads those datoms, so its reads are recorded; a site that must read outside (a process's output) records what it read as the datom the catch-up would write. Audit each of the 80 sites: inside reads move to the store, edge reads stay in the catch-up. Medium. Proof, red first: a reaction over a file's text reruns when that file changes on disk and the catch-up brings it in."
