 {:seon.task/name "admission-counts-memory"
  :seon.task/plan "RESCOPED TO THE OWNER'S RULING (decisions.md 2026-11-16, two rails: \"No declared per-class weights decide admission\"; plan THE FOCUS piece 4: \"Runtime's per-class weights are not built\"). A slice that admitted by declared class weights and a prove-mb dial was built and reverted unlanded on jolt/runtime (1893e8defd, reverted f07bd66660). WHAT IS LEFT, delete-only: (1) seon.fx/held-processes goes: its ps scan costs 75 ms (320k ticks) a pass and answered {\"[^\" 2} at root, counting its own awk pattern, so no reading ever named a batch [M root result/cc647cc9]; with it measured, holds, :peaks, :reading/:fresh, the owner's :held-fn, the SEON_PROVE_BATCH env and its thread (seon.prove/grant's ask -> seon.system's :tests op -> seon.agent/run-tests-forked's 8th arity -> setenv, agent.clj:1343), and :seon.fx/peak-processes, which nothing produces. (2) Admission is the count dials alone (max-workers, max-cores, max-load stay): a runtime test is one fork of its branch's warm process; what a fork holds is bounded by the per-rail memory and tick ceilings at the fork (tests' piece 4; lib/heap-cap! at the child, agent.clj:1340), which end a runaway instead of predicting it. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/admission-reads-nothing-off-the-machine (an owner built with no load-fn reading admits by count alone, and its init names no process reader). The lease (3) is not built: bin/test becomes a client of :prove (tests' piece 6). EARLIER PLAN, superseded where it differs: OWNER: runtime builds, tests reviews (root 2026-11-15 17:1x; the Owner line below is superseded). ORDER: after the update owner lands (owner's order). DECIDED (root): the open item is (b): system tests DECLARE their process count, and a measured peak above the declared count is a problem row, held like an estimate; (a), keeping the ps scan until work reports its peak, is ruled out (a poll). THE PLAN landing 11, admission by weight (owner runtime-2, root approved 2026-11-15). EXTENDS seon.fx's :process owner (admits?, hold-reason) and seon.prove/grant, inside root; no new admitter. (1) Declared weight: an item weighs by its test type (a test child 1, a :seon.test/system-test 3, from tests-4's approved queue design; a tree build or step by its class), with bytes from its class (child memory measured at exit). The owner counts granted weight against max-workers (cores) and bytes against memory-mb. Deletes: held-processes (a ps -axwwE scan, ~70 ms a pass), the SEON_PROVE_BATCH env tags, the measured :peaks, read-machine's load read; the stuck case where ps answers none and nothing is admitted goes. (2) One counter: a grant adds its weight, an answer or a worker's death subtracts it, O(1) a grant. (3) The local path joins it: bin/test (so bin/land --fast) asks the standing root's owner for a lease of shards x weight before forking shards. The lease is held by the client's OPEN CONNECTION and returned by its EOF (seon.server/serve-connection!'s end), a pend entry end-all ends, never by a goodbye alone, so a SIGKILL or crash leaks nothing (root's condition). Estimate: a grant O(1) (~10k ticks: one weight read, one add, one compare); a lease one server op and one pend entry; 0 ps reads (today ~70 ms a pass). Lower bound: O(1) a grant. tests-4's notes (2026-11-15): a bin/test shard is no child (it boots a shared root and runs many tests), so a lease weighs by the shard class's peak, not 1 a shard; the lease op serves only bin/test's shard path and is deleted when bin/test becomes a thin client of seon.agent/test-plan (nothing is built on it); an agent's forked test children (my.agent/prove, run-children!) take the same grant, or agent runs stay outside admission. Open [M, read]: no producer of :seon.fx/peak-processes exists (fx.clj reads it only), so class peaks came only from the ps scan; root decides (a) keep the scan until work reports its peak, or (b) system tests declare their process count. Proofs, red first: seon.fx-test/a-request-past-the-weight-budget-waits; seon.fx-test/a-request-past-the-memory-budget-waits; seon.server-test/a-lease-a-killed-client-held-is-returned. Owner: tests (admission), runtime reviews. What exists [M, read]: seon.fx/hold-reason admits by count (max-workers), the 1-minute load (max-load) and processes (the budget, cores and two); memory is admitted by nothing, though docs/plan.md Stage 1 says work is admitted from the cores and the memory. At 10-100 agents on the 16 GB target memory runs out before cores. Correct: each class's measured peak memory (child memory measured at exit, seon.fx-test/a-child-memory-is-measured-at-exit) weighs a request like its processes, under a memory budget from the machine's free memory less root's, a spec'd config key. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/a-request-past-the-memory-budget-waits."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/admission-reads-nothing-off-the-machine}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-model-call-is-cancelled-at-its-owner"
  :seon.task/plan "Named by flow and context-4 as the one gap of seon.turn/a-turn-is-a-flow-step's :repl side (2026-11-15). What exists [M, git grep at jolt/flow 079eee0b20]: seon.fx has no cancel. A model call (seon.fx/stream-step) ends only on its reader's {:end} or {:error}, or on the owner's quiet bound, which shuts the connection. What changes: root's :stop now ends an agent's run at once (seon.repl/agent-step drops the call in flight; docs/flow.md#flow-inside-an-agent), so the call streams on at the provider and its spend runs to its own end, a paid call nobody reads. Design: the owner takes {:cancel call-id} (sent by the agent's :repl proc when a stop ends a run with a call out, through seon.agent/fx-send!'s path) and closes that call's stream as the quiet bound does, answering the asker's pend with a typed :seon.fx/cancelled, which the asker drops. It stays under the one owner: no second mechanism, and the quiet bound's close is reused. Owner: runtime-2 (effects); flow reviews. Estimate: one message and one close a stopped run, O(1); it saves what the call would have spent past the stop. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/a-cancelled-model-call-is-closed-at-its-owner (a streaming scripted call, cancelled mid-stream, answers :seon.fx/cancelled and its connection is closed; red today: no cancel exists, and the stream runs to its end)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-cancelled-model-call-is-closed-at-its-owner}}
 {:seon.task/name "proving-runs-as-flow"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow (root, 2026-11-15 evening: the self-merge path first; Stage 1's first safety item; runtime's D reviewed by flow). docs/plan.md Stage 1: a test, a process test and a merge's check are messages to the effects owner's procs, admitted from the cores and memory by bounded-channel backpressure, with no admission check beside it; the merge gate's git worktree and rebuild go, and a merge's check is a prove of the candidate branch in the system.\nPRIOR WORK (searched: tasks by subject 'admission', 'as a Flow', 'backpressure'; seon.fx, seon.prove, seon.agent, seon.reconcile, seon.system; flow's notes flow-admission-design-2026-11-15.md), read [M, read 2026-11-15 17:2x]:\n- ONE ADMITTER, ALREADY THE PATH: root's :process effect owner (seon.fx owner-proc; hold-reason fx.clj:1666 holds a classed request by flight count against max-workers and processes against the budget; its in-flight map is the lease table, its bounded in-port parks askers). Landing 11 (seon.fx/admission-counts-memory, D) adds weight and memory to it.\n- AN AGENT'S TEST RUN IS ADMITTED (corrected: the first plan claimed a bypass at agent.clj:1726; there is none): seon.agent/run-tests-forked's one caller is the agent's :tests op (agent.clj:2096), reached only by the :process owner's ask of a batch it admitted (seon.prove grant, prove.clj:195, :seon.fx/ask) or by seon.prove's :plan, which forks nothing (prove.clj:475). An agent's prove! asks seon.prove (agent.clj:1044).\n- THE GATE: a change reaching process tests is judged :gate (reconcile.clj:480-494); root's :gate request (reconcile.clj:916) runs seon.fx/run-tests, the :tests effect with 1 worker (system.edn:77), on a checkout of the merge candidate (run-on-checkout: a git worktree and a build), then seon.system/prove-tree! catches a branch up from that checkout's files and proves it through seon.prove (admitted). So the gate's tests are admitted; its checkout, worktree and rebuild are what the safety item deletes.\n- OUTSIDE ADMISSION: bin/test's and bin/land's local shards (path E, tests' landing 13).\nTHE CHANGE (to plan with its measured cost before code; root reads it): the :gate proves the candidate's store branch in the system: prove! of the task's own branch with its deferred process tests named, on the standing binary when the candidate's binary inputs did not move (seon.system/binary-current), else one build; no checkout, catch-up or git worktree. Deletes: run-on-checkout's worktree and rebuild for the gate, and the gate checkout config, onto prove! of the branch. No functionality removed.\nCOST: to measure first: a gate's run today (worktree, build, catch-up, prove) on a real candidate, against the in-system prove of its branch.\nPROOF (red first): seon.reconcile-test/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch.\nMEASURED and READ (flow, 2026-11-15 17:4x by date): the gate is configured (gate-checkout .worktrees/merges [M result/d45b64e1]) but has NOT RUN SINCE 2026-11-01 (its newest prove run 20261101T125220, its last seon build that day [M ls]); 8 tasks hold deferred process tests, none at :gate now. So the first seon_done that reaches a process test takes a path idle for 14 days. THE DUPLICATE [read fx.clj:1272 run-on-checkout, system.clj prove-tree!]: the gate writes the candidate's changed files FROM the store (seon.writer/written) over a git worktree at the branch's git head, then prove-tree! catches those same files back INTO a new tree branch and proves that: store -> disk -> store, though the task's branch is already in the store. The disk copy exists only because a process test boots a root from files on disk. DESIGN: prove the task's own branch (no tree-branch, no catch-up, no git worktree); a process test's tree is the branch's files written from the store (the one write-back), built only when the branch's binary inputs moved (binary-current). Process tests reading from the store is landing 13's (E, tests): ownership to settle with root and tests before code.\nROOT (2026-11-15 evening): the gate side is flow's (seon.fx run-tests on a checkout, fx.clj:1272, prove-tree!'s round trip), tests reviewing, runtime reviewing the build decision, which is runtime-for, never a new check; E's bin/ clients stay tests'. Prove it on a real branch before the seon_done pilot, its first real use. AGREED with tests (tests-4, 17:5x): ONE read of a tree from the store, written once, called by the gate and E's clients: seon.source/write-tree! over seon.source/files (source.clj:929, {path text} of what db holds as program) and seon.source/source-files (source.clj:391, extended there if a binary's checkout needs more: bin/, resources/seon/config, deps.edn, native/), each written by seon.fx's write-file (stage and rename), into a directory keyed by the branch head's t. flow writes it, tests reviews.\nPLAN WITH COST (flow, 2026-11-15 18:1x by date), for root's read before code:\nMEASURED [M root, warm, twice equal]: seon.source/source-files on main lists 224 files in 356k ticks (result/c300234f); seon.source/files renders them, 8.0 MB, in 235-240M ticks (result/afca172d), ~29 ticks a byte. So a whole tree per gate is the wrong shape (proportional to the change, AGENTS section 3a).\nDESIGN, O(d) a candidate: 1. seon.source/write-tree! writes main's tree ONCE per main head into a directory keyed by that head's t, through seon.fx's write-file, the old one removed when the next is written; 2. a candidate's tree is that directory cloned (APFS clone: metadata only) plus only its changed files, the ones seon.writer/written already names (source/changed-files), written over it; 3. the gate proves the task's own store branch with its deferred process tests named on that tree, on the standing binary when runtime-for says the candidate's binary inputs did not move, else one build (runtime reviews); no git worktree, no tree-branch, no catch-up of files that came from the store.\nCOST: once per main head 240M ticks (the measured whole render); per gate the clone (224 files of metadata) plus d changed files (~29 ticks a byte; a typical change of a few files ~1-5M ticks), against today's git worktree (~5 s for a land checkout [M target/land/20261115T164312-jolt-flow.log.checkout]) and its whole catch-up. Lower bound: d files a candidate.\nDELETES: run-on-checkout's bin/worktree --at for the gate, prove-tree!'s tree-branch and catch-up for the gate (prove-tree! stays for E's sessions until their clients call write-tree! too), and the gate checkout .worktrees/merges. No functionality removed.\nPROOFS (red first): seon.source-test/a-tree-written-from-the-store-matches-the-branch (red: no write-tree!); seon.reconcile-test/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch (a change reaching a process test is proved on its own branch, with no worktree made).\nREVISED (root's review of 0e489f6763, 2026-11-15 evening; it replaces the PLAN WITH COST's write-tree! and its DELETES of .worktrees/merges): write-tree! would be a SECOND writer of main's files from the store, beside the one write-back. PRIOR WORK it missed [read]: my.root/write-back! renders main's changed files from the store (doc/files over the paths the merged tasks wait on) and commits them on the merges branch, in .worktrees/merges, through one :git effect, so that tree is kept current per main head; bin/update uses it, and plan item 14 (data, runtime) owns it. DESIGN: the gate's base is the write-back's tree; a candidate's tree is that tree cloned (APFS clone: metadata only) plus the branch's changed files (seon.writer/written's :files) rendered by the same code (doc/files), on the standing binary when runtime-for says its binary inputs did not move, else one build; the gate proves the task's own branch on it. No new writer, no write-tree!, no git worktree, no tree-branch or catch-up of files that came from the store. COST unchanged per gate: the clone plus d changed files (~29 ticks a byte, result/afca172d); the base costs the write-back it already pays. DELETES: run-on-checkout's bin/worktree --at for the gate, prove-tree!'s tree-branch and catch-up for the gate. .worktrees/merges STAYS (the write-back's). The interface agreed with tests is the same: their clients clone the same tree. Owner's rule (relayed by root): a script's outside logic is never ported; design the job inside, or delete the script.\nOPEN, before code (flow, read 2026-11-15 evening): the write-back runs after merges and repeats lost ones (write-back!), so .worktrees/merges can lag main; a clone of a lagging tree plus only the branch's own changes would give a process test a wrong tree. O(d) and right: the write-back records the main t its tree holds, and a candidate's files are those changed between that t and the candidate's head (main's moves since plus the branch's), rendered by the same doc/files. Asked of data (plan item 14's): does the write-back record its main t today, or is that the extension?\nRESOLVED (data agreed, 2026-11-15 evening): the write-back records its main t, :t on :seon.system/source (source-moved-tx), built on jolt/flow 1c718e82bc, data reviewing. So a candidate's files are those changed between the source fact's :t and the candidate's head: main's moves since (d/changes from :t) plus the branch's own (seon.writer/written), rendered by doc/files over the clone of the write-back's tree.\nSTATUS (flow, 2026-11-16 05:3x): STEP 1 ON MAIN. The gate proves the candidate's own store branch (seon.system/prove-candidate!, 5e4c5d0ddb), and ends the agent it spawned (cfad440a16). A red gate keeps its checkout's target/prove (items' build and seed logs) beside its log. Pilot run 15 merged through it at 04:4x. STILL OPEN: the git worktree and root's binary clone stay (run-on-checkout) until tests' E settles how a process test gets its program; then the clone of the write-back's tree replaces them (step 2). The findings of root's review of step 1 are seon.fx/the-gates-candidate-path-is-its-own. The seon.reconcile-test/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch proof is not written; step 1's proofs are seon.fx-test/a-gate-is-proved-by-the-system-on-its-candidates-checkout and seon.system-test/a-gates-agent-ends-with-its-answer.\nRED-FIRST PROOF FOR STEP 2 (2026-11-16): seon.fx-test/a-gates-checkout-is-a-clone-of-the-write-backs-tree: a gate run makes its checkout by cloning the write-back's tree (the merges tree at the source fact's :t) and writing over it the files changed since that :t plus the candidate's, with no bin/worktree call and no git worktree left registered. Red today: run-on-checkout calls bin/worktree --at. It replaces seon.reconcile-test/the-gate-proves-the-candidate-branch, which step 1 met through seon.fx-test/a-gate-is-proved-by-the-system-on-its-candidates-checkout. Waits on tests' E: how a process test gets its program from that tree."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-gates-checkout-is-a-clone-of-the-write-backs-tree}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.fx{run-tests {:n 5, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 2000000}}
