 {:seon.task/name "a-git-subprocess-ends-at-its-declared-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (found by flow's review of slice 2a, 2026-11-15 23:5x). Every git the system runs goes through seon.fx/git-run (fx.clj:61), which calls seon.lib/sh (lib.clj), and sh has no bound: a git that hangs (a lock held by a dead process, a network remote, a pager) parks the :git owner's one worker forever, and every write-back and update merge behind it, and a caller that derefs the effect (my.root/merges-caught-up!, write-back!) on a :server-op worker never gets its answer. The rule (AGENTS 4): a wait on the world outside has a declared, spec'd timeout and ends typed. Change: seon.lib/sh takes a bound (a config key, :seon.config.source/git-ms, with its schema entry and reason), and a child past it is ended (its process group) and answered :seon.lib/shell-timed-out naming the command; git-run passes the git bound. PRIOR WORK, to read first: seon.lib/sh (Jolt's sh on a fiber), how Jolt's sh exposes the child to end it, and any bound another sh caller already declares. COST: none on a git that answers; one timer a call. Proof, red first: a git that never answers (a fake git on PATH that sleeps) ends at the bound with :seon.lib/shell-timed-out. PRIOR WORK FOUND (runtime, 2026-11-16 00:0x): Jolt's clojure.java.shell/sh has no timeout; it parks until the child exits, then reaps it (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/clojure/java/shell.clj:74-81). Jolt's Process does support (.waitFor p timeout unit) and .destroyForcibly, a SIGKILL (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/process.ss:2246-2251). So the bound is buildable in seon.lib/sh: start the process, drain out and err on fibers as sh does, (.waitFor p ms TimeUnit/MILLISECONDS), and past it .destroyForcibly, answering :seon.lib/shell-timed-out. This is shared machinery (every Seon shell): plan, review (flow, deps) and an end-to-end run through each path that shells (git, bin/ scripts, builds) before it lands."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-shell-past-its-bound-ends-typed}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.lib{sh {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 50000, :bytes 100000}}}

 {:seon.task/name "git-is-reached-only-through-its-effect-owner"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (root, 2026-11-16, the owner's order: every external system essential and inside Seon, or ripped out). Git is the one outside program data's area runs. (1) The write-back, seon.fx/git-commit through the :fx.git effect owner from the reconciler's write-back: essential (everything kept reaches git), already the edge done right; it stays. (2) A boot's catch-up (seon.system/catch-up: seon.fx/changed-since, git's diff of main's tree against the checkout, and seon.fx/messages-since, git log for a landing's Completes:) and (3) a session's import (my.branch/import!: seon.fx/files-at) call git and the checkout directly, not through the effect owner.\nPLAN: (2) and (3) ask the :fx.git owner as every effect is asked, one owner for git; both are deleted once sessions work through their agents (edits on their branch, never a checkout read). docs/deps.md's table says git is reached only through :fx.git.\nORDER: after data's three worst designs.\nPROOF, red first: a boot's catch-up and an import read the checkout through the :fx.git owner (no direct call of seon.fx/changed-since, files-at or messages-since outside it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-catch-up-reads-git-through-its-effect-owner}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
